    {
      "case": "unit_1_scope",
      "question": "For Unit 1, what exact required textbook sections and reading scope should I read? Where do I submit the applied reading connection in Canvas?",
      "request_mode": "fresh conversation; Canvas delivery; lesson unit-01.html; no learning field",
      "status": 200,
      "content_type": "text/event-stream",
      "answer": "**Required reading for Unit 1: one textbook, one short section**\n\n- **Book:** Artificial Intelligence: Foundations of Computational Agents, 3rd edition, by David L. Poole and Alan K. Mackworth (Cambridge University Press, 2023)\n- **Section:** 1.1, What is Artificial Intelligence?\n- **Scope:** Read only the opening of section 1.1. Stop at the heading **1.1.1 Artificial and Natural Intelligence**. Don't follow links or read later subsections.\n\nUnit 1 assigns no other textbook sections. The AI Fluency Framework one-page guide is optional further reading, not a textbook assignment. The Unit 1 page has the official section link.\n\nI can't see the book's wording, so I can't summarize it for you. The course explains why this section matters: a useful AI capability is a task you can observe and check, and a fluent answer alone doesn't show the task was done correctly.\n\n**Where to submit the connection in Canvas**\n\n1. Finish the lesson and reading.\n2. Click **Canvas Next** to open the Unit 1 practice and check-in quiz.\n3. Answer the three choice questions.\n4. Put your project update in the final written question. This is the zero-point written question, so it doesn't add to your grade.\n5. Click **Submit Quiz**.\n\nThe reading connection belongs in that same project update. It should name the book and section, explain one idea in your own words, and state the decision it informs. It isn't a separate assignment.\n\nWould you like to draft your one-idea explanation here first? Share it and I'll point out where it's clear and where it needs a specific decision.",
